1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,683
This is STAT 331, video 9. The topic is the estimate of the mean response in simple linear regression.

2
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:15,490
This video prepares you for these question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:25,369
Type R is a short recap: inference for beta one from computer output. Tutorial 2, question 1, parts a to c, asks it.

4
00:00:25,819 --> 00:00:33,563
Type A: derive the mean, the variance, and the distribution of mu zero hat, the estimate of the mean response.

5
00:00:34,013 --> 00:00:45,533
Type B: compute mu zero hat and a confidence interval for the mean response. Tutorial 2, Assignment 1, and the practice questions of lecture 4 ask it.

6
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:51,167
Type C: explain the width of the interval, and the shape of the confidence band.

7
00:00:51,617 --> 00:01:10,988
First, a short recap. The model says y i equals beta zero plus beta one x i plus an error. The errors are I I D normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared. The four assumptions are linearity, independence, normality, and equal variance.

8
00:01:11,438 --> 00:01:27,289
The least squares estimates are beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat times x bar. For the brainhead data, the green line is the fitted line.

9
00:01:27,739 --> 00:01:43,355
When we put sigma hat in place of sigma, beta one hat minus beta one, over its standard error, has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. This gives the confidence interval and the test for beta one.

10
00:01:43,805 --> 00:02:01,597
For the brainhead data, the standard error of beta one hat is 0.01307. The 95 percent confidence interval for beta one is from 0.2351 to 0.2866.

11
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:11,348
Question type R recaps videos 7 and 8. Tutorial 2 asks it in parts b and c. Method card R has four steps.

12
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:19,841
Step 1: read beta one hat, its standard error, sigma hat, and the degrees of freedom, n minus 2, from the output.

13
00:02:20,291 --> 00:02:36,632
Step 2: to test beta one equals 0, compute t equals beta one hat over its standard error. The p-value is the probability that the absolute value of T is at least the absolute value of t. The red tails show it.

14
00:02:37,082 --> 00:02:51,183
Step 3: the confidence interval is beta one hat plus or minus the t quantile times the standard error. The marked points minus t and t cut off the central area 1 minus alpha.

15
00:02:51,633 --> 00:02:56,945
Step 4: conclude in words. Use the units of the problem and the word mean.

16
00:02:57,395 --> 00:03:17,598
Now Tutorial 2, question 1. A hospital measures satisfaction, y, and illness severity, x, for 25 patients. The question gives x bar, 50.48, S x x, 532.24, and the R output. Video 10 covers parts e and f.

17
00:03:18,048 --> 00:03:42,389
Pause the video and try it. Part a asks for the fitted line. Step 1 of card R: read the Estimate column. Beta zero hat is 179.1312 and beta one hat is minus 2.3283. So y hat equals 179.1312 minus 2.3283 x.

18
00:03:42,839 --> 00:04:04,557
The fitted line passes through x bar, y bar. So y bar is 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 50.48. That is 179.1312 minus 117.53, which is 61.60.

19
00:04:05,007 --> 00:04:30,692
Read also sigma hat, 13.52, on 23 degrees of freedom. 23 is n minus 2, so n is 25. As a check, 13.52 over the square root of 532.24 is 0.5860. This matches the standard error of the slope, 0.5859, up to rounding.

20
00:04:31,142 --> 00:04:49,617
Pause the video and try it. Part b asks if satisfaction depends on severity. Step 2 of card R: write it as a test. The null hypothesis is beta one equals 0: no linear relation. The alternative is beta one is not 0.

21
00:04:50,067 --> 00:05:07,069
Under the null hypothesis, beta one hat over its standard error has a t distribution with 23 degrees of freedom. At the 5 percent level, the critical values are minus 2.069 and 2.069.

22
00:05:07,519 --> 00:05:19,274
The observed value is minus 2.3283 over 0.5859, which is minus 3.974. It is far in the left tail.

23
00:05:19,724 --> 00:05:36,961
The p-value is two times the probability that T is at least 3.974. The output gives 0.000601. This is less than 0.05, so we reject the null hypothesis.

24
00:05:37,411 --> 00:05:44,601
Step 4, conclude: there is strong evidence that the mean satisfaction depends on the severity of the illness.

25
00:05:45,051 --> 00:06:02,373
Pause the video and try it. Part c asks for an interpretation and a confidence interval. Step 4 of card R gives the sentence: for each 1-unit increase in severity, the estimated mean satisfaction is 2.33 units lower.

26
00:06:02,823 --> 00:06:11,527
Step 3: for a 95 percent interval with 23 degrees of freedom, the t quantile is 2.0687.

27
00:06:11,977 --> 00:06:26,655
The interval is minus 2.3283 plus or minus 2.0687 times 0.5859. The half width is 1.212. The blue bar shows it.

28
00:06:27,105 --> 00:06:36,598
So the 95 percent confidence interval for beta one is from minus 3.540 to minus 1.116.

29
00:06:37,048 --> 00:06:47,160
The whole interval is below 0. 0 is not in it. This agrees with the test in part b. Now we ask a new question about the line itself.

30
00:06:47,610 --> 00:06:53,989
The gray dashed line is the true line, beta zero plus beta one x. We do not know it.

31
00:06:54,439 --> 00:07:04,167
At each x, the responses are normal around the true line. The orange curves show this spread. The mean of y at each x is on the true line.

32
00:07:04,617 --> 00:07:21,171
Pick any value x zero. The mean outcome there is mu zero. Mu zero is the expected value of y zero, given x zero. It equals beta zero plus beta one x zero. It is a parameter, so it is unknown.

33
00:07:21,621 --> 00:07:32,821
We estimate it with the fitted line. Mu zero hat equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero. It is the height of the fitted line at x zero.

34
00:07:33,271 --> 00:07:53,325
The fitted line is not the true line, so mu zero hat is not exactly mu zero. The red segment shows the difference. Tutorial 2, question 1 d, asks for mu zero hat at severity 55 and a confidence interval. For the interval, we need the distribution of mu zero hat.

35
00:07:53,775 --> 00:08:00,559
Start from the definition: mu zero hat equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero.

36
00:08:01,009 --> 00:08:20,465
Put in the formula for beta zero hat: y bar minus beta one hat x bar. In the picture, the purple point is x bar, y bar. For the brainhead data, it is 3637.86 and 1284.263.

37
00:08:20,915 --> 00:08:32,392
Collect the two terms with beta one hat. Mu zero hat equals y bar, plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar. This is the second form.

38
00:08:32,842 --> 00:08:52,959
Use it for the brainhead data at x zero equal to 4500. x zero minus x bar is 4500 minus 3637.86, which is 862.14. The blue arrow shows this step along x.

39
00:08:53,409 --> 00:09:11,457
The rise along the line is beta one hat times 862.14. 0.26082 times 862.14 is 224.863 grams. The orange arrow shows it.

40
00:09:11,907 --> 00:09:26,563
So mu zero hat is y bar, 1284.263, plus 224.863. That is 1509.126 grams.

41
00:09:27,013 --> 00:09:37,573
At x zero equal to x bar, the second term is 0, so mu zero hat equals y bar. Card A uses this second form in step 1.

42
00:09:38,023 --> 00:09:48,071
Slide 13 asks: what happens at x bar? Here is an illustration with our own simulation. The dark dashed line is one true line.

43
00:09:48,521 --> 00:09:58,185
We draw a sample of 10 points from this model and fit a line. Then we do it again with a new sample. Each sample gives a different green line.

44
00:09:58,635 --> 00:10:17,921
Here are 15 fitted lines. They cross near the middle, at x bar. The second form explains why. Mu zero hat is y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar. An error in the slope is multiplied by the distance x zero minus x bar.

45
00:10:18,371 --> 00:10:32,728
So the lines are close together near x bar, and far apart at the edges. The red bars show the spread. The estimate of the mean is most precise at x bar. The variance formula will show this exactly.

46
00:10:33,178 --> 00:10:49,071
Section 3. Question type A asks you to derive the mean and the variance of mu zero hat, and its distribution. Lecture 4 does it on slides 14 to 20. Slide 18 asks why mu zero hat is normal.

47
00:10:49,521 --> 00:10:59,143
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: use the second form, y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar.

48
00:10:59,593 --> 00:11:06,675
Step 2: write mu zero hat as a sum of c i times y i, where the weights c i are constants.

49
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:14,805
Step 3: for the mean, use linearity of the expected value and the fact that both estimates are unbiased.

50
00:11:15,255 --> 00:11:31,298
Step 4: for the variance, the y i are independent. So the variance is sigma squared times the sum of the c i squared. Expand the square, and use the fact that the deviations x i minus x bar add to 0.

51
00:11:31,748 --> 00:11:46,596
Step 5: mu zero hat is normal, because it is a linear combination of independent normal variables. Put sigma hat in place of sigma to get a t distribution. Then solve for the confidence interval.

52
00:11:47,046 --> 00:11:50,502
Now we use the card on the slides of lecture 4.

53
00:11:50,952 --> 00:12:08,424
Now card A, step 3: the mean of mu zero hat. Mu zero hat is random, because it comes from the random sample. Its density is the green curve. Line 1: E of mu zero hat is E of beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero.

54
00:12:08,874 --> 00:12:19,839
Line 2: expectation is linear, and x zero is a constant. So this is E of beta zero hat, plus E of beta one hat times x zero.

55
00:12:20,289 --> 00:12:30,871
Line 3: video 6 showed that beta zero hat and beta one hat are unbiased. So the result is beta zero plus beta one x zero.

56
00:12:31,321 --> 00:12:38,830
Line 4: that is the definition of mu zero. In the picture, the center of the green curve is mu zero.

57
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:43,568
So mu zero hat is an unbiased estimator of mu zero.

58
00:12:44,018 --> 00:12:53,533
In repeated samples, each sample gives one value of mu zero hat. The blue marks are such values. They are centered at mu zero.

59
00:12:53,983 --> 00:13:10,644
Now the variance. Step 1 of card A: start from the second form. The variance of mu zero hat is the variance of y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar. The next line changes the two marked parts.

60
00:13:11,094 --> 00:13:28,353
Step 2: write both parts as sums over the y i. Y bar is the sum of 1 over n times y i. Beta one hat is the sum of x i minus x bar, over S x x, times y i. Video 6 showed this form.

61
00:13:28,803 --> 00:13:48,707
Put the two sums together. Mu zero hat is a weighted sum of the y i, with weight c i. C i equals 1 over n, plus x i minus x bar times x zero minus x bar, over S x x. The weights use only the x values, so they are constants.

62
00:13:49,157 --> 00:14:09,893
Look at the weights for a small data set: x equals 1 to 5. Then x bar is 3 and S x x is 10. At x zero equal to x bar, the second part is 0. Every weight is 0.2. The sum of the squared weights is 5 times 0.04, which is 0.2.

63
00:14:10,343 --> 00:14:23,548
At x zero equal to 5, the weights are minus 0.2, 0, 0.2, 0.4, and 0.6. The point at the far end gets a large weight.

64
00:14:23,998 --> 00:14:45,715
The sum of the squared weights is 0.04 plus 0 plus 0.04 plus 0.16 plus 0.36. That is 0.6, not 0.2. Step 4 shows that the variance is sigma squared times this sum. So large weights give a large variance.

65
00:14:46,165 --> 00:15:00,587
Step 4 of card A. Line 1: the y i are independent, and each has variance sigma squared. So the variance of the sum of c i y i is the sum of c i squared, times sigma squared.

66
00:15:01,037 --> 00:15:11,703
Line 2: square each weight. The square of a sum of two parts gives three terms: the first part squared, the second part squared, and two times the product.

67
00:15:12,153 --> 00:15:23,332
Line 3: sum each term over i, and take the constants out of the sums. The first term gives n times 1 over n squared, which is 1 over n.

68
00:15:23,782 --> 00:15:37,585
Line 4: the sum of x i minus x bar squared is S x x. So the second term becomes x zero minus x bar squared, over S x x squared, times S x x.

69
00:15:38,035 --> 00:15:47,656
Line 5: the cross term contains the sum of x i minus x bar. This sum is always 0. So the cross term is 0.

70
00:15:48,106 --> 00:15:52,095
Line 6: cancel one S x x in the second term.

71
00:15:52,545 --> 00:16:14,967
The result: the variance of mu zero hat is sigma squared times the quantity 1 over n, plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. Check it on the small data set at x zero equal to 5. 1 over 5 plus 4 over 10 is 0.6. That equals the sum of the squared weights.

72
00:16:15,417 --> 00:16:35,641
Step 5 of card A. Slide 18 asks why mu zero hat is normal. Line 1: mu zero hat is a sum of c i times y i, with constant weights. The y i are independent and normal. A linear combination of independent normal variables is normal.

73
00:16:36,091 --> 00:16:47,653
Line 2: so mu zero hat is normal, with mean mu zero from step 3, and the variance from step 4. The orange arrows show one standard deviation.

74
00:16:48,103 --> 00:17:01,735
Line 3: subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation. The result has a standard normal distribution, on the z scale. But sigma is unknown, so we cannot use this directly.

75
00:17:02,185 --> 00:17:13,769
Line 4: put sigma hat in place of sigma, by the same logic as for beta one hat. The result has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

76
00:17:14,219 --> 00:17:21,558
The denominator of line 4 is the standard error of mu zero hat. Card B uses it in step 3.

77
00:17:22,008 --> 00:17:40,333
Now solve for the interval. Line 1: the t quantile t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2, cuts off area alpha over 2 in each tail. So the statistic is between minus t and t with probability 1 minus alpha, the green area.

78
00:17:40,783 --> 00:17:55,311
Line 2: multiply all three parts by the standard error. It is positive, so the inequalities stay the same. The distance between mu zero hat and mu zero is at most t times the standard error.

79
00:17:55,761 --> 00:18:14,812
Line 3: subtract mu zero hat, and multiply by minus 1. This turns the inequalities around. Mu zero is between mu zero hat minus t times the standard error, and mu zero hat plus t times the standard error. The blue bar shows this interval.

80
00:18:15,262 --> 00:18:39,283
So a 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval for mu zero is mu zero hat plus or minus t times sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. It has the same shape as the interval for beta one: estimate, plus or minus t, times standard error.

81
00:18:39,733 --> 00:18:49,653
Section 4. Question type B asks for the estimated mean response and a confidence interval for it. Method card B has five steps.

82
00:18:50,103 --> 00:18:59,661
Step 1: the estimate, mu zero hat, equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero. The second form gives the same number.

83
00:19:00,111 --> 00:19:09,007
Step 2: compute the two terms under the square root: 1 over n, and x zero minus x bar squared over S x x.

84
00:19:09,457 --> 00:19:15,366
Step 3: the standard error is sigma hat times the square root of the sum of the two terms.

85
00:19:15,816 --> 00:19:23,773
Step 4: find the t quantile with n minus 2 degrees of freedom, from a table, from R, or from the question.

86
00:19:24,223 --> 00:19:37,279
Step 5: the interval is mu zero hat plus or minus t times the standard error. In the answer, say that it is for the mean of y, for all units with x equal to x zero.

87
00:19:37,729 --> 00:20:04,012
Here is the example of slide 21, with the brainhead data, at x zero equal to 4000 cubic centimeters. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: 335.4323 plus 0.2608207 times 4000 is 1378.715 grams. The ring marks it on the line.

88
00:20:04,462 --> 00:20:14,446
Step 2, the first term: 1 over 236 is 0.004237. The blue part of the bar shows it.

89
00:20:14,896 --> 00:20:33,925
The second term: x zero minus x bar is 4000 minus 3637.86. Square it and divide by S x x. The result is 0.004279. The orange part of the bar shows it.

90
00:20:34,375 --> 00:20:49,031
Step 3: the sum of the two terms is 0.008516. 72.35 times its square root is 6.677 grams. This is the standard error.

91
00:20:49,481 --> 00:20:57,311
Step 4: the t quantile with 234 degrees of freedom is 1.9702.

92
00:20:57,761 --> 00:21:15,062
Step 5: 1.9702 times 6.677 is 13.155. So the interval is 1378.715 plus or minus 13.155.

93
00:21:15,512 --> 00:21:34,755
The interval is from 1365.56 to 1391.87 grams. The blue bar shows it. We are 95 percent confident that the mean brain weight of all people with head size 4000 is in this interval.

94
00:21:35,205 --> 00:21:58,949
Now Tutorial 2, question 1 d. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card B: 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 55. 2.3283 times 55 is 128.0565, so mu zero hat is 51.0747.

95
00:21:59,399 --> 00:22:04,263
Step 2, the first term: 1 over 25 is 0.04.

96
00:22:04,713 --> 00:22:25,129
The second term: x zero minus x bar is 55 minus 50.48, which is 4.52. The blue arrow shows it. Its square is 20.4304. Divide by 532.24 to get 0.038386.

97
00:22:25,579 --> 00:22:38,208
Step 3: the sum is 0.078386. 13.52 times its square root is 3.7853. This is the standard error.

98
00:22:38,658 --> 00:22:44,738
Step 4: the t quantile with 23 degrees of freedom is 2.0687.

99
00:22:45,188 --> 00:23:00,164
Step 5: 2.0687 times 3.7853 is 7.831. So the interval is 51.0747 plus or minus 7.831.

100
00:23:00,614 --> 00:23:26,257
The interval is from 43.24 to 58.91. The tutorial solution uses R with unrounded inputs and gets 43.25 to 58.91. We are 95 percent confident that the mean satisfaction of all patients with severity 55 is in this interval. The green band shows the interval at each severity.

101
00:23:26,707 --> 00:23:48,765
The next type B problem is from Assignment 1, question 3 d. Pause the video and try it. There are 90 berries. x is the sugar content, and y is the chewiness. Find a 95 percent interval for the mean chewiness at 220 grams per liter. The box gives the numbers that we compute from the data file.

102
00:23:49,215 --> 00:24:16,927
Step 1: use the second form. x zero minus x bar is 220 minus 217.333, which is 2.667. Beta one hat times 2.667 is minus 0.0608. So mu zero hat is 2.7083 minus 0.0608, which is 2.6475.

103
00:24:17,377 --> 00:24:23,009
Step 2, the first term: 1 over 90 is 0.011111.

104
00:24:23,459 --> 00:24:43,470
The second term: 2.667 squared, over S x x, 70657.4, is only 0.000101. In the bar, the orange part is very small, because 220 is close to x bar.

105
00:24:43,920 --> 00:25:06,363
Step 3: sigma hat is the square root of S S E over n minus 2. That is the square root of 74.126 over 88, which is 0.9178. Times the square root of the sum, 0.011212, the standard error is 0.0972.

106
00:25:06,813 --> 00:25:14,173
Step 4: the t quantile with 88 degrees of freedom is 1.9873.

107
00:25:14,623 --> 00:25:31,476
Step 5: 1.9873 times 0.0972 is 0.1932. So the interval is 2.6475 plus or minus 0.1932.

108
00:25:31,926 --> 00:25:44,086
The interval is from 2.454 to 2.841 millijoules. It is for the mean chewiness of all berries with 220 grams per liter of sugar.

109
00:25:44,536 --> 00:26:01,837
The last type B problem is practice question 2 b of lecture 4. Pause the video and try it. The question gives the estimates, S x x, x bar equal to 0, and sigma hat. It asks for a 95 percent interval for beta zero.

110
00:26:02,287 --> 00:26:16,922
Line 1: beta zero is the mean response at x equal to 0. So a confidence interval for beta zero is a confidence interval for mu zero at x zero equal to 0. Card B solves it.

111
00:26:17,372 --> 00:26:24,455
Step 1: mu zero hat is beta zero hat plus beta one hat times 0. That is minus 2.

112
00:26:24,905 --> 00:26:36,339
Step 2: 1 over 10, plus 0 minus 0 squared over 16. The second term is 0, because x zero equals x bar. The sum is 0.1.

113
00:26:36,789 --> 00:26:50,357
Step 3: the standard error is 0.4 times the square root of 0.1, which is 0.1265. This is also the standard error of beta zero hat.

114
00:26:50,807 --> 00:27:04,013
Step 4: n minus 2 is 8. The question says that the probability that T is less than 2.31 is 0.975. So the t quantile is 2.31.

115
00:27:04,463 --> 00:27:18,756
Step 5: 2.31 times 0.1265 is 0.2922. So the interval is minus 2 plus or minus 0.2922.

116
00:27:19,206 --> 00:27:31,259
The 95 percent confidence interval for beta zero is from minus 2.2922 to minus 1.7078. The blue bar shows it.

117
00:27:31,709 --> 00:27:49,395
Section 5. Question type C asks you to explain the width of the interval. Lecture 4 asks it on slides 23 to 25. The green curve is the standard error of mu zero hat for the brainhead data, as a function of x zero.

118
00:27:49,845 --> 00:28:01,791
Step 1 of method card C: write the standard error. Sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x.

119
00:28:02,241 --> 00:28:19,927
Step 2: at x zero equal to x bar, the second term is 0. The standard error is sigma hat over root n, which is 72.35 over root 236. That is 4.71 grams, the smallest value.

120
00:28:20,377 --> 00:28:36,483
Step 3: away from x bar, the second term grows. At 3000 the standard error is 9.57. At 4500 it is 12.21. A larger standard error gives a wider interval.

121
00:28:36,933 --> 00:28:52,507
Step 4: a larger sample size n makes 1 over n small. A larger spread of the x values makes S x x large. The dashed curve shows n and S x x 4 times larger: the standard error is half.

122
00:28:52,957 --> 00:29:02,365
Step 5: the interval is for the mean mu zero, not for single y values. Each single y has its own error epsilon.

123
00:29:02,815 --> 00:29:15,572
Slide 21 computes the interval at many values of x zero, from the smallest head size to the largest. Here are the data and the fitted line. Pause the video and try it.

124
00:29:16,022 --> 00:29:28,715
Join the lower limits and the upper limits. This gives a 95 percent confidence band for the mean response. Slide 23 asks why the band gets wider toward the edges.

125
00:29:29,165 --> 00:29:41,432
Card C, step 2: at x bar, the second term is 0. The standard error is 72.35 over root 236, which is 4.71.

126
00:29:41,882 --> 00:29:54,405
The half width is t times the standard error: 1.9702 times 4.71, which is 9.3 grams. The red bar at x bar shows it.

127
00:29:54,855 --> 00:30:13,329
Step 3: at the smallest x, 2773, the second term is 2773 minus 3637.86, squared, over S x x. That is 0.024406.

128
00:30:13,779 --> 00:30:24,830
Add 1 over n, 0.004237. 72.35 times the square root of the sum is 12.24.

129
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:36,032
The half width is 1.9702 times 12.24, which is 24.1 grams. The red bar at the left edge shows it.

130
00:30:36,482 --> 00:30:45,079
At the largest x, 4747, the second term is 0.040140.

131
00:30:45,529 --> 00:30:53,017
Add 1 over n. 72.35 times the square root of the sum is 15.24.

132
00:30:53,467 --> 00:31:04,219
The half width is 1.9702 times 15.24, which is 30.0 grams. The red bar at the right edge shows it.

133
00:31:04,669 --> 00:31:15,123
So the term x zero minus x bar squared over S x x grows with the distance from x bar. The standard error and the interval grow with it.

134
00:31:15,573 --> 00:31:23,722
Slide 24 asks the second question: why do most of the points fall outside the lines? Pause the video and try it.

135
00:31:24,172 --> 00:31:33,089
Card C, step 5: the band shows where the mean is. Its half width is only 9.3 to 30.0 grams.

136
00:31:33,539 --> 00:31:52,206
A single y is the mean plus its own error epsilon. The standard deviation of epsilon is about 72.35 grams, much more than the half width. So only 41 of the 236 points are inside the band. They are orange.

137
00:31:52,656 --> 00:32:06,587
Slide 25 adds: even if we knew the mean exactly, new points would not fall on the line. To predict a new response, we must add the error of the new point. That is the topic of the next video.

138
00:32:07,037 --> 00:32:27,133
To recap. Card A derives the properties of mu zero hat. It is unbiased. Its variance is sigma squared times 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. It is normal, and with sigma hat we get a t distribution and the confidence interval.

